# .gitignore for Ori Studio.
#
# Ticket ORI-T-0003. Spec anchor: ENV_SETUP §1, LLD §6.
#
# This file is read by agents as well as humans, so every group says why it is
# here. Two standing rules:
#
#   - Never add a pattern that hides a build input. If a file is needed to
#     reproduce a build, it is tracked. See "Deliberately NOT ignored" below.
#   - This file is not a secret control. It stops an accident, not an attacker,
#     and it does nothing about history. The control is the secret scan gate
#     (CI_CD gate 7), which covers tree and history. Treat the .env group below
#     as a second layer, never as the reason a secret is safe.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rust and cargo build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The urgent one. `target/` is regenerated by every cargo invocation and was
# untracked and unignored until this ticket: a single `git add -A` would have
# committed tens of megabytes of build output permanently, and history is not
# rewritable here (CONVENTIONS "Git": no force push, no history rewriting).
# Unanchored so it also catches per-package target directories, for example the
# one a Tauri build leaves under `apps/desktop/src-tauri/`.
target/
# `rustfmt` backup files, from a `cargo fmt --backup` run.
**/*.rs.bk
# Windows debug symbols, emitted next to the binary by the MSVC toolchain.
*.pdb

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate and test tooling output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `cargo-mutants` (CI_CD gate 6) writes its working tree and report here.
mutants.out/
mutants.out.old/
# Coverage instrumentation and reports, from the coverage matrix gate (gate 4)
# and any local coverage run.
*.profraw
*.profdata
/coverage/
lcov.info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operating system noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inherited: `.DS_Store` was this file's only line before this ticket. The rest
# is the same class of file from the other two platforms Ori Studio targets, so
# that a Windows or Linux contributor does not commit theirs either.
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
.AppleDouble
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor and IDE droppings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-developer tool state, never shared. Machine-specific paths in these files
# produce diffs that mean nothing to anyone else.
.idea/
*.iml
.vscode/
.fleet/
.zed/
.helix/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.netrwhist
/tags
.ccls-cache/
.cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# State that LLD §6 puts outside the repository
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LLD §6 places the event log, the projections, the search and vector
# indexes, session transcripts, evidence blobs and the single-writer lock under
# `<app data>/ori/`, not here. Nothing in that tree is needed to reconstruct
# methodology-defined state: `products.rebuild` regenerates it from the
# repository and the event log. These patterns exist because a developer run
# with the app data directory pointed at the checkout would otherwise drop that
# state into the working tree, and because a committed `product.sqlite` would
# put an append-only hash-chained log into a place where a merge could rewrite
# it.
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-journal
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm
# A local `<app data>` root pointed at the checkout, under either of the two
# names the engine accepts. Anchored: only at the repository root.
/.ori/
/ori-data/
# Application-level config from LLD §6. Non-secret, but per machine
# (host connection, defaults, organizational repository path). Anchored so a
# crate's own `app.toml`, should one ever exist, is still tracked.
/app.toml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENV_SETUP §4: no secret in the repository, in `.env` files tracked by
# git, in fixtures, in tests, in logs, in events, in tickets or in reports. No
# agent may read these files at all (CLAUDE.md rule 4); ignoring them keeps an
# accidental `git add -A` from turning a local file into a tracked one.
#
# Unanchored on purpose. A developer's `.env` is as likely to sit beside a
# crate (`crates/ori-broker/.env`) or under `ui/` as at the root, and `/.env`
# would ignore only the root one. Anchoring is therefore the wrong repair for
# the fixture problem below: it would buy one fixture file back at the price of
# every real credential file one directory down.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# The exception, and it is a specification requirement rather than a
# convenience. TESTING §5 gives `fixtures/migrated-with-drift` "a tracked env
# file with fake keys", criterion ORI-P1-024 has `ori migrate` detect it and
# report it by key names and shape only, and ROADMAP phase 1 exits on that
# journey. A fixture whose whole purpose is to be found in the tree has to be
# in the tree. The two patterns above matched it at any depth, so ORI-T-0074
# would have built the fixture, committed every part of it except the one file
# the criterion turns on, and reported success: the "present and reporting
# nothing" class this repository keeps finding.
#
# Scoped to `fixtures/` rather than to that one path, because ORI-T-0074 is
# free to add more of them (`.env.local`, a per-service file one directory
# down) and the gate proofs under `fixtures/planted/` (TESTING §4) will want
# their own, and a per-file exception goes stale in silence, which is the
# failure being repaired here. `/fixtures/**/` matches zero or more
# directories, so `fixtures/<product>/.env` and a nested one are both covered,
# while anything outside `fixtures/` keeps the unanchored rule above.
#
# Widening the exception this far is safe for the same reason the fixture is
# allowed to exist: ENV_SETUP §4 forbids a secret in a fixture outright, the
# values there are fake by specification, and the control that would catch a
# real one is the secret scan gate (CI_CD gate 7) over tree and history, never
# this file.
#
# One limit, stated so nobody debugs it twice: git cannot re-include a file
# whose parent directory is excluded, so a fixture env file placed under a
# directory another group here ignores (`target/`, `node_modules/`, `dist/`)
# stays ignored. That is the right answer for build output inside a fixture,
# and it is the one case where ORI-T-0074 has to move the file rather than
# amend this rule.
!/fixtures/**/.env
!/fixtures/**/.env.*
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
# Per-OS release signing material (`SIGNING_KEY_<OS>`), which lives in the CI
# secrets store and never in a checkout.
/signing/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# UI build, Node and pnpm
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ENV_SETUP §1: Node and pnpm are for the UI build only and are never a
# runtime dependency of the engine. Their output and caches are regenerated
# from `package.json` and the lockfile, so none of it is tracked. The lockfile
# itself is, for the same reason `Cargo.lock` is.
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/
.pnpm-debug.log*
dist/
.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logs and temporary files from local runs and from `scripts/`. An agent's
# report belongs in the PR or in `ops/` through the reporting tool, never as a
# stray file here.
*.log
*.tmp
/tmp/
/scratch/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT ignored. Do not add these.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Cargo.lock`: this workspace produces a binary (`ori`), so the lockfile is
#   part of the build. CI_CD gate 7 runs `cargo-audit` and `cargo-deny` against
#   it, and CONVENTIONS "Rust" says versions are pinned by `Cargo.lock`. An
#   ignored lockfile would leave both the reproducible build and the advisory
#   gate reading whatever resolved that day.
# `rust-toolchain.toml`: the toolchain pin is a tracked build input, same
#   argument.
# `.claude/agents/`: generated from `spec/agents/*.md` (ORI-T-0008) but tracked,
#   because it is what the runtime actually loads.
# `fixtures/`: the fixture products are load-bearing test inputs for the
#   end-to-end suite and for CI_CD gate 8, not examples and not scratch. Their
#   env files are named back in by the exception in the credentials group,
#   because TESTING §5 requires a tracked one with fake keys.
# `ops/`: structured operational records written by the engine. Tracked.
